Admittedly it has been a long time but I do believe gcc got taso which was
added to gnu-ld prior to that.  And since the kernel had support before that
you could work magic with dd and set a flag in the ELF header but all it
gained you was a guarantee that your addresses would have the top 32 bits be
0.  Also interesting is that the alpha architecture or kernel appears to
guarantee you get a 64 bit address if the flag is clear by setting at least
one bit in the high 32 bits.

