30 April 2003

Old DAC960 Controllers and Their Use on Alpha Machines under Linux
==================================================================

Older DEC Alpha machines were often equipped with DAC960 cards that
were OEMed from Mylex, but had their own custom firmware produced by
DEC.

Version 2.70, the last custom firmware revision to be released for
these older controllers, was not in the list of firmware revisions
originally supported by the DAC960 driver in the 2.4.x Linux kernels,
version 2.4.11, dated 11 October 2001, author Leonard N. Zubkoff.

With one small patch to that driver, to accept firmware version 2.70
on the DAC960 controller, the DEC custom firmware appears to work
quite well. Although there are some extraneous and misleading messages
that do appear when running with this older firmware, the driver and
controller seem to function reasonably well, allowing the DAC960 disks
to be used to boot Linux from SRM console.

The 2.4.18-27.7.x.hp version of the Linux kernel has the
above-mentioned patch, along with a patch necessary to allow the
DAC960 disks to be mounted as root (/).

The only older cards tested successfully were several versions each of
the DAC960 PD and PU controllers, named by DEC the KZPSC and KZPAC,
respectively, and having the Manufacturer Numbers (from Mylex),
usually on a sticker on the back of the board, of:

DEC Name	Mylex Manufacturer Number
--------	-------------------------

KZPSC		D040347 (1 channel)
KZPSC		D040348 (2 channels)
KZPSC		D040349 (3 channels)

KZPAC		D040395 (1 channel)
KZPAC		D040396 (2 channels)
KZPAC		D040397 (3 channels)

Version 2.70 of the DEC custom DAC960 firmware (swxcrfwp.270) is
available for public download, along with the firmware FLASH utility
(ra200fl.exe), RAID Configuration Utility (ra200rcu.exe), and
associated documentation, from:

    http://www.compaq.com/alphaserver/products/storage/ra230/

The FLASH and Configuration utilities are run from the ARC or
AlphaBIOS consoles on Alpha machines. A typical setup is to put
the files ra200fl.exe, ra200rcu.exe, and swxcrfwp.270 onto a DOS
format floppy, and "run" them from there under console control,
as per the documentation.

Note that there is no SWCC client available for Linux, only for
Tru64 and OpenVMS.

WARNING!

It is not recommended to attempt FLASHing Mylex firmware onto a DEC
DAC960, nor to FLASH DEC custom firmware onto a Mylex DAC960. A dead
board may result.

Good luck!


