> portables are just as proprietary (and some are more so) and no 
> cheaper.

I like the idea of a low power, high performance processor for a note book
computer, but I have to disagree with the assertion that the P4 notebooks
are no cheaper than Apple notebooks.  I am currently using a Sony VAIO
that I got while shopping for a Powerbook G4.  Feature for feature,
the VAIO matched or bettered the PB, with the exception of the Altivec
co-processor, for about $1000 less.  That extra $1k must have gone to
pay for that rather useless titanium skin.  That price difference is
just too much to pay for the cute decorations in the case and the OS.
And the Ti Book STILL can't capture DV without dropping frames, like
any of the VAIOs.

My other problem with the G4 (and now G5) processors is that it takes some
serious assembly programming to fully exploit the Altivec coprocessor.
It's like the days of running PDP-11's and VAX's with attached array
processors; Your application only benefits if it maps into the functions
available in any of the Altivec optimized libraries.  If you need to
perform double precision calculations, it can not help you, and that
covers most of the scientific community.  I thought we came out of those
dark ages of writing microcode to extract that last increment of
performance out of a piece of hardware, as much fun as they were.


