On Tue, 2003-06-24 at 01:41, William H. Magill wrote:
> Also... Remember what killed the Alpha -- quantity, or rather, the lack 
> thereof.
> IBM has a much larger internal demand for Power chips than DEC ever had 
> for Alphas ... but Apple has a demand that is probably an order of 
> magnitude greater than IBM itself!

Yep.  Apple has found a renewed set of marketshare.  Portables were
their smartest move.  Even PC Portables have the highest profit margins,
because they are largely proprietary and OEMs make a killing on
accessories.  Apple found it found "play" in that space easily.  Now
with the PowerPC 970, they have more teeth.  I mean, with Intel, you've
either got the P3 (Centrino) or the slower P4-M.

Back to the desktop, IBM's entry into the commodity Power chip space
will only bring down the prices as well.  It seems IBM's strategy is to
sell the commodity version for Linux solutions (as well as to Apple). 
If the mainboard and chips are cheap enough, I might just have to switch
my main home workstation over to it -- possibly number crunchers as well
as LAMP servers at work too.

> That means the per chip price is dirt cheap... which will work its way 
> up through the entire Foundry... Expect AIX based "servers" to drop in 
> price while increasing in performance.

I don't know if IBM's going to use it on its AIX line much, other than
maybe cheap workstations.  I think they are going to continue to produce
a "high-end, high-fabrication cost, high tolerance" version of Power
chips for AIX servers and most workstations.

> And at $3K for a box with two 2ghz cpus and a 1ghz system bus from 
> Apple that implies that IBM might offer a "midrange server" in the 
> under $10K range that will offer price performance that will simply 
> kill SPARC virtually overnight, not to mention Dell's foray into that 
> market. ... and it will offer upward compatibility into the rest of the 
> Power line.

Between PowerPC 970 and AMD Opteron, I really don't know how Intel-only
server companies can survive in the server and 4GB+ RAM workstation
market.  Xeon's performance stinks at 4GB+ RAM and Itanium is far too
expensive.

> Couple this with the fact that IBM DOES have decent compiler 
> technology, significant experience with emulation from the 68K to 
> PowerPC migration, and, unlike Digital, Compaq or HP, DOES understand 
> Marketing -- Itanium with Alpha inside is likely to prove too little 
> too late.

I think I'm going to use that, "Itanium with Alpha inside."  That's a
good one.

> And also remember, Intel has said that Itanium would not be offered for 
> desktop machines.

Correct, not until 2009 (or now 2007 I thought I heard them change?).


