On Tuesday, June 24, 2003, at 02:45  AM, Eugene Chu wrote:
> From: "William H. Magill" <magill@mcgillsociety.org>
> Date: Tue, 24 Jun 2003 01:41:31 -0400
>
>> And also remember, Intel has said that Itanium would not be offered 
>> for
>> desktop machines.
>
> Was this a recent announcement?  My HP rep is working on providing me
> with an evaluation Itanium workstation next month.

It was part of the original Itanium Road Map, especially from HP... 
nominally two years+ ago now.

However, there is still a big difference between a "Workstation" and a 
"Desktop" machine, especially the way HP/Q defines and markets them. 
... about a $5-10K difference.

Many of us bitched and moaned for years to Q about the lack of a 
"desktop" Alpha. The closest we ever got was the XP1000 "Workstation," 
but that was still too bloody expensive to be considered a "desktop." 
[And remember, a 4100 comes in a "desk side" enclosure also!]

Intel's basic statement has always been that the Itanium architecture 
would not replace the X86 architecture for the desktop market. (Or at 
least not as long as the X86 continued to be a cash cow, with margins 
much larger than they can ever hope to see from Itanium.)

Needless to say, any manufacture can create a box, put whatever they 
want into it and call it a "desktop." But the "market" defines a 
desktop machine as something in the $1,000 and under range. [Unless you 
are Apple and then you can get folks to pay $2K and under.:)]

HPTC and even Education is a serious niche market as far as HP/Q is 
concerned, but a niche market none the less. In my previous life, I had 
an XP1000 667 as a workstation, but at $18,000 educational, that was 
the only one in the office. Everybody else either had Wintel boxes or 
some variety of SparcStation. Together those six machines cost less 
than the XP1000.  Itanium suffers from the same problem.

